00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.429 align:start position:0%
 
the Trump administration may increase  the number of United States troops in  Afghanistan this week defense secretary  James Madison is granted authority to  set those levels retired four-star  General Stanley McChrystal commanded  American and international forces in  Afghanistan he also served as commander  of the Joint Special Operations Command  McChrystal was tasked with reimagining  the battlefields of the Middle East the  outlined how he did that in his 2015  bestseller team of team new rules of  engagement for a complex world one of  his co-authors would form a Navy SEAL  Chris bustle  Russell has now Pollard that up with a  book of his own the book is called one  mission how leaders build a team of  teams the book shows how people in any  field can apply general McChrystal's  lesson to their organization good  morning to both of you Laura  There is obviously concern around the  nation we're going to see the baseball  game this evening and we saw this  tragedy happen yesterday in a sense of  because you think about national  security you think about this country  and you think about the timely  conversation what was your reaction when  you watch that yesterday and what we  need to be thinking about well I think  like everybody else it's horror at what  happened but it's also the hope that it  starts a national conversation about a  number of issues one is partisanship one  is about conversation itself and another  is about why so many people are being  killed by guns in our country and I  think we need thoughtful people to come  together and take on a tough issue you  know Chris that's what struck me  yesterday you know we've been reporting  on it all morning 5,200 shots but when  you actually hear the gunshots and you  hear how long that it took and what  those people must have been going to you  know as a Navy SEAL you know the sound  what were you thinking when you were  looking at that and what it all means  well obviously sympathy for the folks  are there in the poorest families and  and the the broader problem here which I  think Stan just identified that this is  this is increasing and that we have to  get our hands around this this issue and  for people that haven't been exposed to  that before I mean that's that's a  life-changing moment they'll wrestle  with for years sort of leadership that  we need in situations like this and what  sort of lessons do you think general can  be taken from the book well I think the  first is it's not all about a single  leader it's about a community of P  they come together because when we are  together we get more brains involved we  get more wisdom involved we get  different perspectives and we can make  decisions that actually have some kind  of consensus and support so if we always  look for a single iconic person I think  we make a mistake you right Chris about  the leadership myth hmm what does that  mean like you're [ __ ] man well it's in  our nature we have this tendency to want  to look for one person that will lead us  through tough times we saw that in the  military with our senior generals maybe  they'd say well we got the right general  in place but you always hear in business  you can only have one leader well you  have one leader but what great leaders  have always done and what they is more  important now than ever that they do is  they really bring teams together so they  create environment where we could  connect that was what was critical for  us in the military and I think that's  what's important now in industry and  politics you see it all over the  leadership is leadership is leadership I  mean whether it's in the military  whether it's in business whether it's in  politics that's right that's right what  are the core principles of that kind of  leadership well in today's world I think  it's more important than ever that  leaders create an environment where  those that are closest to these very  difficult problems in the world is being  driven by networked individuals and  interconnected problems leaders have to  empower those that are closest to those  issues to be able to move with autonomy  that's what worked for us in the service  and that's what we see working well in  industry as folks start to apply that  sort of model what did you take from  what you learned on the battlefield how  that can be translated into businesses  because most of us don't have your  skills or your skills general we're not  Navy SEALs and we're not equipped to  know to do the things that you do but  you said there are things that you do on  the battlefield that really can  translate into business I've been out of  the service for five years now and I've  been constantly impressed by the  similarities between the service and  industry there's great leaders there's  great people in those organizations that  want to do great things I just want to  be part of something that has deeper  meaning and that they feel attached to  and that's what was critical for us in  transitioning how special operations  worked and I think there's a lot of  similar characters in industry Chris  gentlemen Chris I'm Admiral McRaven was  here recently he's got a book he's  talking about which is about lessons  he's learned about starting with making  your bed but one of the interesting  things he talked about in terms  of Navy SEALs was that you often  surprised by those who make the cut  were you surprised it really is  interesting you'll have a someone that  just finished an Ironman Triathlon who  doesn't make it through the second week  of training and you know a 19 year old  kid from Iowa was never seen the ocean  who ends up being the top graduate so  it's really about inner character rather  than physical skill which is also about  leadership absolutely but what did you  see in him general because I loved how  you described him is one of your  favorite words charlie intellectually  curious that there was something about  him that stood out to you out of all the  people that you worked with when I was  first commanding and he was an  Operations Officer one of the  subordinate task force's Chris was  asking different questions he was asking  big questions he was asking how the  pieces fit together not about the focus  narrow things and that of course makes  an immediate impression one question  about the Secretary of Defense mattis  the president seemed to have given him  lots of authority is that a good thing  or do we mean be do we need to be  reminded you know that the heart of the  American system is civilian control I  think it is a good in order to empower  subordinates who are closest to the  problem to make decisions but you don't  abrogate responsibility so the president  of United States still has  responsibility for those decisions and  the American people ultimately still  have ownership of those decisions so it  has to be a melded system in which  civilians and military leaders together  arrive at right decisions but also  prosecute policies that everybody  supports that everyone understands  ultimately they may not agree going in  but when the policy is set we all have  the resolve to follow through general  the war in Afghanistan has been going on  since 2001 where should troop levels be  it's hard to say I certainly would defer  to what Jim mattis and McNichol son on  the ground are recommending but the  question I think we need to ask  ourselves is if we send more troops and  that doesn't solve the problem or make  that the change we hope what will we do  then and that's a question that the  nation needs to ask itself about its  long-term aims and objectives in  Afghanistan and what we're willing to  devote to achieve them alright thank you  so much great to see you good to see the  two of you think Ilana thank you and of  course is Stan Lee  Christel the general and Chris Russell  the name of the book is one mission it's  on sale now wherever you like to buy  your books

